    tip: "Think of an arrow from reason to result. *So* points forward to the result: *I was hungry, so I ate.* *Because* points back to the reason: *I ate because I was hungry.* Same two ideas, turned around.",
    note: "In everyday American speech, *so* often starts a sentence or a story (*So, how was your trip?*), and *'cause* (sometimes written *cuz* in texts) is a casual *because*: *I left 'cause I was tired.* Keep these for speech and messages to friends, not for school or work writing.",
    myth: "Myth: “Never start a sentence with *and, but* or *because*.” Good writers have always done it: *But that was only the start.* A sentence that starts with *Because* is fine as long as it has both parts: *Because it rained, we stayed in.*",
